ROADMAP.md:427:| R-0502 | One value/raw-memory boundary and one layout authority | the boundary is named in the theorem; layout has one owner with its proofs; the default struct layout is declaration order, natural alignment defined exactly per target, size rounded up to the alignment, zero-sized fields taking no bytes, with no C ABI promise | R-0496 |
ROADMAP.md:457:| R-0575 | The per-function facts report and query from slice 0 ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-facts-report), 2026-11-06) | every function of each slice has a report derived from the Lean rules, with a versioned schema: capabilities and the grants and resources actually reachable, allocation and allocator, trusted, foreign and opaque boundaries with their assumptions, each operation that could fail and how it is handled, termination (evidence with its named premises, or the responsible source permission and the call path to it), recursion depth and stack bound or why none is established, proof status per obligation, and completeness, each with its reason, evidence and source location; a permission that justifies nothing is reported as a fact, which a `Concrete.toml` policy may refuse (the decided refusals of an unmatched `#[proof]` and a duplicate `#[may_not_terminate]` stay) (carried from slice 0 into CheckedCore and the schema, spec obligations O1 and O4); the schema already has fields for what memory a function writes (frames) and what its outputs depend on (dependency flow), reported "not established" until derived: definite, maybe and never writes per `&mut` place and the output-to-input dependencies (control dependence, cleanup and opaque calls included) are derived into these fields as soon as `&mut` lands, not in E–G, while R-0524, R-0473 and R-0383 keep source frames, contracts and package summaries; facts are preserved across indirect calls, specialization and package boundaries (R-0588); from slice 0 the compiler answers a query about one function or module from the same facts, as readable text and in the machine format (the command is `concrete facts`); the report never claims what the rules do not establish; every fact is a typed judgment in Lean with a derivation-soundness theorem (and obligation-discovery completeness where it claims absence), one derivation feeds the readable view, the machine format and every policy, each fact has a closed status (established, conditional, refuted, not established, not applicable) with per-fact coverage, unknown never satisfies a policy, and answers carry typed identities, versioned schema and semantics and artifact digests; from slice 0 the report also carries the build identity (compiler SHA, toolchain pins and target), each program's maximum stack use, measured from the final binary on both backends, and the trusted-base statement naming the Lean kernel, LLVM printer and LLVM, LCB's MIR→assembly step, linker, OS and named runtime premises as not proven ([early decisions](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-early-evidence)); the diagnostics and facts JSON schema is frozen and versioned from the first front-end output, with golden tests; a planted wrong fact and a planted unknown version are caught; Home: spec (the stack-bound claim and, from slice 0, the refusal of an unknown or over-budget bound against the `Concrete.toml` stack budget) and tooling (the report). Gate: stack, the final-artifact bound checked against the claim and the budget ([tracking](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-tracking)) | R-0499 |
ROADMAP.md:461:| R-0594 | Zero-heap facts proved in slice 0 ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-memory-2026-11-06)) | every slice-0 function's heap facts are 0, established by the derivation (no allocator is reachable), not by schema defaults; a planted reachable allocator flips them | R-0575, R-0592 |
