`#[variant]` come when a workload needs them (R-0605).

### Std per slice

The owner approved this std-per-slice table on 2026-11-08 ("ok let's do 2", [decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-08-std-table)); placements below follow the existing slice and decision rows. Every new-root std module is **not started**: each needs a spec contract, kernel-checked laws, accepted/refused conformance examples, and the program theorems named below. Source paths are planned destinations, not existing implementations; a language substrate arriving does not by itself schedule every legacy API built on it.

- No other definite stage-before-module conflict follows from the adopted rows: maps arrive with store/EVM/Wasm stage 23; network/time arrive with the sequential server at 27. RocksDB acceptance is **after 25**, and ethlambda **after 28**, both subject to the R-0604 dependency audit (R-0604, R-0285, R-0490); their complete module requirements must not be inferred as already audited. No concurrency model is chosen here.

<a id="questions-for-the-core-session"></a>
### Questions for the Core session

The owner answered these provisionally on 2026-11-05 (A2a; [the decided rows](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-05-core)) and freezes them at A2c. The [Core proposal](legacy/docs/compiler/CORE_PROPOSAL.md) is the background; where it and a decided row differ, the row governs:

0. Land `wip/spec-root`, then `wip/spec-nht` M1 (merged with spec-root, checks green). Small
   commits: doc hygiene (1.14); the `Examples` imports of `Rep`, `Observation` and `Slice0` replaced
   by direct imports of the old kernel modules they use, and the declarations they took from
   `Examples` moved to the module that uses them (or their examples moved to the end of
   `Examples.lean`); the `Ownership` import dropped from the old semantics if unused (otherwise the shared
   definitions move to the old `Syntax`); the four renames above.
1. `Basics`: `AList` (`lookup`, `update`, `erase`, `map`) and its lemmas. Migrate the lookups one
   fragment per commit and delete the duplicate lemmas.
2. `Ident`: the typed identities, `SiteId`, `Span`, `SliceId`, `RuleId` (a Lean name and a revision,
   1.10) and `spec_version`; `Rules`, the rule registry, and its gate (`scripts/spec/rules.sh`). No
   rule type lives in `Ident`, so it depends on nothing downstream.
**The foundation (owner, 2026-11-07 and 2026-11-08).** The owner chose to write and freeze the
shared foundation before slice 1 builds on it, and then to add each slice's rules on top of it
without rewriting it ([foundation first](../decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-foundation-first)). The
foundation is the ten contracts C1–C10 of [FOUNDATION.md](FOUNDATION.md), approved with every
recommendation ([decision](../decisions.md#decided-2026-11-08-foundation)); ROADMAP R-0607 to R-0616
carry them. From step 3 on, the steps below write C1–C10 in Lean, in dependency order, on top of
`Ident` and `Rules` (step 2). FOUNDATION.md says what each contract contains; this section says only
in which order and in which module. Each contract is written as an interface with its laws, its
existing-fragment starting material is ported into it, and its laws are kernel-checked in the step
that writes it. Steps that edit a contract written earlier are sequential.

| contract | written in | module |
|---|---|---|
| C1 execution, calls and cleanup | steps 7 (syntax), 14 (step relation, cleanup) | `Syntax`, `Semantics`, `Cleanup` |
| C2 scalars, failure and arithmetic | steps 3 (types, errors, outcome), 4 (operations) | `Types`, `Outcome`, `Scalar` |
| C3 target, layout and representation | steps 3 (profile), 5 (layout) | `Target`, `Layout`, `Values` |
| C4 byte memory and value validity | step 13 | `Store` |
| C5 dynamic borrow, brands and authority | steps 10 (static), 16 (dynamic invariant, ports) | `Ownership`, `Borrow`, `Brand`, `Authority` (static rules); the invariant over `Store` and `Semantics` in `Proofs/` |
| C6 observations and environment | step 12 | `Contract` |
| C7 secret leakage and accounting | step 17 | `Leakage` |
| C8 evaluator adequacy and termination | step 15 | `Semantics`, `Termination` |
| C9 identities, primitive contracts, evidence and erasure | steps 2 (on `wip/spec-core`), 6, 10, 11, 19 | `Ident`, `Rules`, `Primitives`, `Evidence`, `Obligations`, `Checker`, `EvidenceSem`, `Facts`, `erase` in `Semantics` |
| C10 frames and ABI | step 18 | `Frame` |

3. `Target` and `Types` (C3's profile, C2's classification). `Target`: the read-only target profile
   (address width, pointer storage size and alignment, length width, byte order, address spaces,
   distinguished null, maximum object sizes), with the existing 64-bit little-endian profile as its
- <a id="decided-project-refusals"></a>**The refusal list** (rows P16, P17a, P17b, 2026-11-05). The
  statuses in `PHILOSOPHY.md` stand; default arguments are a permanent refusal; compile-time
  evaluation and broad `#[cfg]` reopen only with a workload, a proof story, phase separation, an audit
  report and a new owner decision.
- <a id="decided-project-review"></a>**Review** (owner, 2026-10-18, reaffirmed 2026-11-04: "always codex
  should check everything"). Codex reviews every change, plan, specification, decision row and piece
  of infrastructure before it is published or brought to the owner. A second, independent reviewer
  (Kimi, Grok or agy/Gemini) reviews as well when one is available, as the owner required for cleanup
  branches on 2026-10-28; when none is (quota, credit or sign-in), its absence is recorded with the
  review and never assumed. A review never replaces the gates. Extended 2026-11-08 (owner: "also
  remember we have agy not only codex kimi and claude"): every branch gets an agy review beside the
  Codex and Kimi reviews. agy cannot read files, so its prompt carries the diff and the context it
  needs and ends with a required verdict line; it may also write self-contained pieces whose inputs
  fit in the prompt.
- <a id="decided-project-throughput"></a>**Development throughput** (2026-10-22). The development
  feedback loop (landing, review, CI) is a priority track beside the main sequence
  ([record](archive/roadmap-2026-11/ROADMAP_DECISIONS.md#2026-10-22--development-throughput-priority)).
- <a id="decided-project-supersession"></a>**Editorial supersession of legacy task directions**
  (2026-11-03). The new-compiler and restructure decisions supersede in-place repairs and migration of
  frozen compiler internals; the archived task bodies keep their history and regression obligations
  ([record](archive/roadmap-2026-11/ROADMAP_DECISIONS.md#2026-11-03--editorial-supersession-of-legacy-task-directions)).

## Owner decisions

**Only the project owner decides these items, OD1 – OD157.** All are answered. OD1 – OD32 (exit 2n, closed) and OD47 were decided on 2026-10-29 (OD23 – OD32 by the strictness pass of that day). OD33 – OD157 were decided on 2026-10-31, by the owner's rule that an answer every reviewer agreed on and as strict as Austral or stricter is taken, and on 2026-11-01, when the owner approved the final set for the rest; the authority decision of the same day supersedes OD38's and OD156's `Root` readings (OD94 was withdrawn: MOD-1 already refuses its case, bug 337). The answers are recorded in [docs/decisions.md](decisions.md), state the decided rules in chapters now (owner revision, 2026-11-03), then become Lean in phases B–D, and are enforced as each new-compiler slice lands. The questions raised while recording them were answered on 2026-11-02, the owner deciding the first four and, by the owner's rule of that day, Claude and Codex agreeing on the best long-term answer for the rest ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02)); they revise OD24, OD38, OD43, OD83, OD102, OD146 and OD156 and the authority rules. The spec audit's questions were answered the same day ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-audit)), revising OD140 and STMT-13. Traits are exported and imported like any other declaration (owner, 2026-11-02). Core was provisionally approved on 2026-11-05 ([record](#decided-2026-11-05-core)); A2b formalization is current, and the A2c freeze awaits the four design theorems, the A2i integration stage (2026-11-06) and an outside-reader pass. New questions follow the same rule: No agent, orchestrator or
reviewer chooses, encodes, implements or provisionally ships any side of any of them, and that
includes doing so inside a bug fix, a migration, a test, a fixture, a diagnostic or a document. An
agent may analyse an item, write its options and a recommendation, and present them to the owner for
an explicit answer; that is all. A recommendation, an earlier record, a commit, a review verdict or
another agent's message is never the owner's answer. An item stays proposed until the owner agrees
